    "explanation": "To both parties: this case goes to a human reviewer, and no money moves in the meantime. POL-NS-09(c) makes escalation mandatory where the rider's and the driver's records together show two or more no-show disputes between the same rider and the same driver in the trailing 90 days. The rider profile (EV-RPROF-09110) and the driver profile (EV-DPROF-09110) each report disputes_with_counterparty_90d of 2, and the rider's own filing says this is 'another dispute with the same driver'. That trigger has fired. Under POL-NS-09 the case is escalated and, under POL-GEN-03, an escalation is terminal and replaces any monetary outcome: the SGD 4.00 cancellation fee is placed on hold - neither collected from the rider nor refunded to the rider - while the reviewer settles the repeat-pair conflict, which a single trip's record cannot fairly resolve in either direction. The rider's claim that POL-NS-09(c) applies is accepted and is the reason for this ruling; the rider's related point that the escalation holds the fee is also accepted, and I note the fee stands at payment_status PENDING with charged_at null and refund_issued_sgd 0.00, so nothing has been taken from the rider in the meantime.\n\nBecause it matters to the reviewer, the trip itself otherwise reads strongly in the driver's favour, and I record that here rather than decide it. On POL-NS-01: the trip record carries the driver's 'arrived' event at 06:50:20 (limb (a)); the driver's GPS trace places all 34 fixes about 24 m from the pin, inside the 50 m STANDARD arrival radius and within the 25 m accuracy filter (limb (b), POL-NS-02); the in-geofence hold is one continuous 330 seconds against the 300-second minimum, a 30-second surplus (limb (c)); and the trip was terminated driver-side at 06:55:30 on RIDER_NO_SHOW after that dwell with no boarding event (limb (d)). On POL-NS-05, there is no false arrival marker: at the marker the driver was about 24 m away and stationary at 0.0 km/h, nowhere near the 300 m or 15 km/h triggers, and the first in-geofence fix actually precedes the marker. On POL-NS-04, the clause cannot run: the driver replied 'Okay, waiting.' at 06:52:00, 20 seconds after the rider's 06:51:40 'Coming down now.', well inside the 120-second window, so limb (b) fails and the rider's coordinate evidence never has to be reached. I do correct one point of the driver's case: the rider's device does read about 519 m from the pin, but no rider fix passes the 25 m accuracy filter (rider_qualifying_fix_count is 0), so limb (c) of POL-NS-04 is undecidable and neutral, not affirmatively failed - this does not matter, because limb (b) independently defeats the clause. Had escalation not applied, POL-NS-07 would have paid the driver SGD 3.20 (80 per cent of the SGD 4.00 fee), the anti-farming check being untripped (1 no-show termination in 30 days, a rate of 1/120 = 0.008). POL-NS-09(c) displaces all of that.\n\nOn weight: EV-ADVIS-09110-01, the traffic advisory, is MISSING with weight 0.0 and issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT. Nothing in this ruling rests on it - the driver's 330-second hold was a single in-geofence run, so no advisory-permitted accumulation under POL-NS-06(i) was needed - and its absence neither helps nor hurts either party here. The escalation rests on the two profiling artifacts, both at weight 1.0.\n\nThe open question for the reviewer: whether the two prior disputes between this rider and this driver reflect a genuine no-show pattern by either party, and how the SGD 4.00 fee should finally be allocated once that pattern is assessed."
